==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|scharte}}, from {{inh|de|goh|scart}}, {{m|goh|*scarti}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*skardą}}. Probably related to Indo-European ''*sker-a-'' "to [[cut]]", and thus related to German {{m|de|scheren}}. Akin to Dutch {{m|nl|schaarde}}, English {{m|en|shard}}, Old Norse {{m|non|skarð||notch, hack}} ( > Danish {{m|da|skår}}).

